# The other half of the inertness bracket: a capability that is inert at zero must
# still DO something when turned up, or "inert at 0" is trivially true because the
# feature is dead. 06 proves it costs nothing off; this proves it is not off-by-being-
# broken.
#
# ✅ CONFIRMED on the first Mac run (2026-08-05): diff_frac=0.008427 against tol 0.004.
# Surface FX does visibly apply to the Standard material at this roughness, so the
# claim in the docs is now tested rather than asserted. (It was written as an
# unverified prediction; that is what it stopped being.)
#
# Reference is 06-fx-baseline rather than 01-original-standard for the reason in
# 06-fx-inert's header: the rotation-phase accumulator is not reset between scenes, so
# a distant reference measures the clock as well as the feature. Adjacent is exact.
#!desc      Surface FX turned up must visibly change the frame (else it does nothing).
#!checks    nonblack
#!expect    differs-from 06-fx-baseline
#!tolerance 0.004

generator "organic math"
surface original
material standard

set rot_mod_x 0 rot_mod_y 0 rot_mod_z 0
set rot_amp_x 0 rot_amp_y 0 rot_amp_z 0
set trans_amp_x 0 trans_amp_y 0 trans_amp_z 0
set scale_amp 0
set cam_path 0 cam_speed 0 cam_kick 0

set exposure 0 metallic 0.1 roughness 0.5
set ambient 0.3 glow 0.2 bloom_intensity 0.2

# Both FX driven hard — if neither moves a pixel, that is the finding.
set iridescence 1.0 irid_scale 2.0 irid_shift 0.3
set subsurface 0.8 sss_distortion 0.5 sss_power 2.0
